Subject: [PATCH 15/15] rdna-boosts: block 15: the streamed KV -- a fixed-VRAM
 resident WINDOW (Goal A) and an attendance POOL (Goal B), both opt-in
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Goals A and B of TODO #118, promoted from the `wip/kv-residency` campaign.  This block is the delivery's
"the KV does not have to be O(-c) of VRAM" work: TWO opt-in placements of the same cache, and each one is
byte-identical to a build without it when its knob is unset.

  * the WINDOW (`LLAMA_KV_RESIDENT_CELLS=<cells>|auto`): the newest W cells stay in VRAM, everything older
    spills to pinned system RAM in the SAME VA reservation -- a positional window, no kernel or op change;
  * the POOL (`LLAMA_KV_REGION_POOL=<cells>`): the authoritative copy of the WHOLE context in a pinned host
    mirror, and a compact device pool of the pages a decode step actually attends to, which the QSA sparse
    read resolves through a page table (`GGML_OP_KV_POOL`).

They are mutually exclusive by construction (the pool declines while a window is active) and they answer the
same question from opposite ends: the window keeps the *newest* cells hot, the pool keeps the *attended* cells
hot.  Both report `is_streaming()`, so a streamed cache takes the sparse QSA decode arm (B2, below).

==========================================================================
PART 1 -- the WINDOW (Goal A, promoted in r178)
==========================================================================

Goal A of TODO #118, promoted from the `wip/kv-residency` campaign.

WHAT IT DOES.  The KV cache's VRAM cost becomes a WINDOW of the newest `W` cells instead of `-c` cells: one
VA reservation (a CUDA/HIP VMM region) holds the whole cache, the window's cells are backed by VRAM and
everything below them by pinned system RAM.  `set_rows`, the in-place rope shift, `state_write/read`, the kq
mask and the FA kernels keep addressing one contiguous range, so no kernel and no op changes.  The window
slides with the watermark in whole map units, so a remap happens once per few thousand tokens and the
apparatus itself is free (a zero-spill arm measures the same as plain).

WHY.  (a) A context that OOMs today runs: 27B Q8_0 `-c 262144` (8704 MiB of KV) now runs on one 32 GiB card
at 128 MiB VRAM + 8576 MiB pinned host for the KV.  (b) With host-resident MoE experts, the VRAM the KV does
not need *yet* goes to the expert-cache arena: measured at `-c 262144`, `-n 2000`, `-ncmoe` = the model's
full layer count, 51 433-token prompt, W at or above the run's depth = **+2.2 to +2.9 GiB of arena** (+3.9 to
+6.7 points of residency) for free -- 122B-A10B Q4_K_XL +3.8 % decode and +4.7 % prefill, 35B-A3B Q8_0 -0.9 %
decode and +3 % prefill; a `W` 35 k cells BELOW the run depth costs 11-26 % (the spill is read every token).

OPT-IN, AND ONLY WHEN ASKED FOR.  `LLAMA_KV_RESIDENT_CELLS=<cells>` (a number) or `=auto` (`--fit` sizes it,
and only spills a context the user pinned with `-c`); unset/0 = no window.  With no window the KV takes the
PLAIN device buffer, so a run without the knob is byte-identical to a build without this block -- including
its OOM (verified).  `LLAMA_KV_SPILL_RAM_PCT` (85 % of MemAvailable) caps the pinned spill and widens the
window rather than being OOM-killed; `LLAMA_KV_REGION=0` disables the provider; `LLAMA_KV_REGION_QUARANTINE=0`
and `LLAMA_KV_REGION_DEBUG/TRACE/CANARY/DUMP` are the diagnosis switches.

PURITY (the window).  A spilled KV is numerically transparent: prefill-logit **KLD -0.000000 / 100.000 %** on a spilling
27B arm and -0.000000 / 99.994 % on a spilling Flash-Next (two KV caches: the attention KV and the QSA
indexer), text byte-identical on 27B (f16 and q8_0), 4B, 35B-A3B and Flash-Next, and the state paths, the
context-checkpoint path and the MTP verify widths all green on arms that provably spill.  Two driver hazards
are held: a kind flip zeroes a neighbouring live host mapping unless the released physical is held across the
create (`LLAMA_KV_REGION_QUARANTINE`, default on -- `=0` reproduces the corruption), and every tensor in a
windowed region is placed on the driver's map granularity so a slide step coincides with a mapping.  An
idle-slot park is REFUSED on a region-backed cache (the server then saves the slot to the prompt cache and
clears it, which is byte-identical to the parking gate's reference) because a park moves cells above the
region's mapped watermark through the copy engine, which faults the GPU.

FIT.  `--fit` sizes and reports the window (`auto`), or honours and reports a numeric `W`: the resident/spill
split, the VRAM saved, and the per-token read cost at the model's full context from the backend's calibrated
link rate.  The fit identifies the KV's bytes by the region buffer TYPE, and its measurement context is a
`no_alloc` probe that declines a window -- so the probe takes the region buffer explicitly
(`llama_kv_region_set_probe`); without that the fit cannot see the KV, reports an over-budget config and
aborts instead of sizing the window (the r178 regression).

GATES (1 GPU, gfx1201): default path byte-identical (`5ba44647188f`) and still OOMs; VMM selftest PASS and
FAIL with the hold disabled; windowed arms 305 `e2cea6fa6670` = plain at W=1024/2048/4096 (spilling at 1024:
128 MiB host), `QUARANTINE=0` -> 325 `fb65430049e9` (the hazard); `--fit` sizes `auto` (96680 of 262144
cells, 3210 MiB resident) and reports a numeric W; `gate-ckpt-reuse.sh` / `gate-state-pipe.sh` / MTP verify
widths on spilling arms; `test-backend-ops -o FLASH_ATTN_QSA` 26/26; `check-gpu-faults.sh` clean.

Not in this block: the campaign's Goal B (a sparse attended set) and Goal C (the MTP drafter ring,
`LLAMA_SPEC_DRAFT_WINDOW`) -- both remain campaign work -- and A1 (the superseded two-base FA read).
Records: `wip/kv-residency/A2-MIXED-BACKING.md` (§3.7.9, §3.7.11), `HANDOVER.md`.

MULTI-DEVICE `--fit` (r181; the window itself reaches a tensor split in r204, at the end of this message): the
KV-region glue is now safe on a Meta (tensor-split) device, and `auto`'s decision reaches the cache as a
decision.  r178 made default `--fit` abort on ANY `-sm tensor` run with more than one device: the fit's KV
probes and the cache's own region lookups call `ggml_backend_dev_backend_reg(dev)` and hand it to
`ggml_backend_reg_get_proc_address`, and a Meta device has no backend reg (`device->reg == NULL`), so
`GGML_ASSERT(reg)` fired at model load.  Three paths asserted: the fit probe (any run), the runtime buft
lookup (a numeric W), and the spill budget/link lookups (a numeric W below `-c`).  r181 makes a null reg read
as "not provided" and declines the window with one message naming the tensor split (fit and cache), keeping the
KV fully resident -- which is the r181 state; r204, below, is what makes the backend answer there.

Three wiring defects in the same code, all visible on ONE device:
- `auto`'s "no window needed" decision (0) was indistinguishable from "nobody decided", so the cache warned
  "--fit did not run" immediately after the fit had decided exactly that.  `llama_kv_region_set_resident_cells`
  now records that a decision was made (`llama_kv_region_resident_cells_decided`), and a decided 0 is silent.
- every run without the knob logged "the windowed KV is unavailable for this cache (no spilled cells)".
  That message now only fires when a window was actually requested and declined for a real reason.
- the pinned-RAM ceiling clamp could raise W past the VRAM budget and then be reported as "does not make this
  fit either".  It is now only taken while the wider window still meets the VRAM target; otherwise the fit
  says the ceiling cannot be served at this `-c` and drops the window (which the fit then fits by reducing the
  context).  That ordering is not cosmetic: an intermediate form of this fix accepted a 235 GiB pinned spill
  plan for a 4B at `-c 8388608`, and creating it OOM-killed the test box.

GATES (the window, gfx1201): `--fit on` (default) at 2 and 3 devices, `-sm tensor`, with no knob / a numeric W / `auto`,
fit on and off: no assert, tokens produced, exactly one message per requested window.  `-sm layer` 2 devices
and 1 GPU still take the window per device (numeric W), and `auto` still sizes the 27B Q8_0 `-c 262144` q8_0
arm identically (123265 of 262144 cells, 4092.8 MiB resident / 4611.2 MiB spilling).  One device, no knob: no
KV message at all.  Purity unchanged: plain vs a SPILLING windowed arm prefill-logit KLD 0.000000 / 99.990 %
(q8_0 KV), greedy text byte-identical on that arm; 35B-A3B (1 GPU and 2-GPU `-sm tensor`), dense 27B and the
canonical 27B Q8_0 prefill-logit gate (0.000000 / 100.000 %) byte-identical to r180; MTP Protocol A unchanged
(tensor acceptance 0.60816 = 1937/3185, plain == draft-mtp); no GPU faults.

==========================================================================
PART 2 -- the ATTENDANCE POOL (Goal B, promoted in r190)
==========================================================================

The attention KV lives twice: its AUTHORITATIVE copy in a pinned host MIRROR (cell-indexed, so the writers,
state_write/state_read, the K-shift, defrag and seq_park all work on it unchanged -- the writer rule) and a
compact DEVICE POOL of pages of one indexer block (compress_ratio cells) with a page table block -> slot and
its inverse.  A decode step resolves its top-k selection through the table and fills the pages it missed from
the mirror; the QSA reader gathers K/V at the pool slots while the mask stays cell-indexed.
LLAMA_KV_REGION_POOL=<cells>, unset/0 = off = byte-identical.

Contents.

- ggml/src/ggml-cuda/kv-pool.{cu,cuh}: GGML_OP_KV_POOL.  MARK sets use[slot] = epoch for every page this
  launch reads AND every page the batch just wrote (the graph's k_idxs), and queues the written ones for
  re-fill; CLAIM takes a slot for each DISTINCT missing block (the table CAS is the dedupe) and never takes a
  slot MARK protected; MAP+FILL writes each selected cell's pool position into the reader's K/V id list and
  copies the claimed and dirty pages from the mirror, K and V sharing slots.  The epoch is a device counter
  incremented once per launch: the graph bakes pointers, never counters, and nothing is allocated per launch.
- ggml-cuda.cu: the pool's pinned host mirror buffer type -- a DEVICE buffer type whose allocation is pinned
  host memory (`ROCm_Host` reports is_host, which would send the KV's writers and the FA read to the CPU on a
  discrete device), and its proc-address entry.
- llama-kv-cache: the eligibility, the per-layer pool tensors (pool_k/pool_v, tbl, inv, use, one shared
  counter block), the resolve wiring into the QSA id list, kv_pool_serves() (a batch too wide for the pool
  takes the cell-indexed path), the teardown accounting (LLAMA_KV_POOL_STATS takes a PATH: a line on stderr
  sits inside the generated text), and memory_breakdown() charging the mirror to the host bucket so `--fit`
  does not reserve it as VRAM.
- llama-memory-hybrid-idx / llama-memory-hybrid / llama-memory: the second id list (the K/V gather follows
  the page table while the mask stays cell-indexed), the flag threading, and the pool's byte accounting for
  `--fit` (llama_get_kv_pool_bytes()).
- models/qwen4exp.cpp: a pooled cache reports is_streaming() and the sparse arm uses the pool's id list; the
  MTP draft's own cache is never pooled (its attention is dense).
- llama-context / the async state copies: the mirror's buffer type is accepted by the batched save and restore
  (its bytes are host memory, staged through the pinned state_stage).
- common/fit.cpp + src/llama-ext.h: the pool's split in --fit's arithmetic and report (the mirror is pinned
  host RAM, the pool is VRAM), and no context-reduction interpolation while the pool is active -- the pool's
  VRAM is fixed, so reducing -c frees nothing and the interpolation would divide by a near-zero
  bytes-per-context delta.

Invariants, each one caught by the greedy text.

1. MARK then CLAIM: a claim must never evict a page the same launch reads.  Without MARK the round-robin
   victim could be a page another row still needs (the text diverged ~400 chars in, and a pool larger than
   the whole cache hid it completely).
2. Claim ATTEMPTS are not distinct blocks: a slot per id attempt wrapped the cursor within one 4-row batch, so
   two blocks shared a slot and a just-resolved block went unresident.  The table CAS dedupes first, so only
   distinct blocks consume a slot and kv_pool_serves() bounds the distinct count.
3. No transient scratch in a captured graph: the fill list lived in a ggml_cuda_pool_alloc that is freed at the
   end of the op, and a captured graph bakes the pointer, so a replay wrote into whatever op had been handed
   that block ("values verify clean, text differs run to run").  It lives in the counters tensor.
4. The mirror is AUTHORITATIVE: the writers land in it and a declared page is re-filled from it before the
   attention reads it, so an eviction costs nothing and nothing has to be invalidated -- except by a path that
   rewrites cells in place without going through the writers (the K-shift, a state restore), which calls
   kv_pool_invalidate().
5. Eligibility is narrow on purpose: one stream, non-MLA, K and V both present, !v_trans, a uniform
   compress_ratio and cell geometry, no window active, a device-offloaded cache, and only the trunk context of
   a qwen4exp model.  Anything else declines with one message and the KV is unchanged.
6. The pool is floored at one decode band's worth of blocks (8 x ceil(width/page) pages) so a verify batch
   cannot exhaust the slot budget, and the mirror is bounded at creation by LLAMA_KV_SPILL_RAM_PCT (it is
   pinned, non-swappable RAM) -- over the ceiling the pool is declined loudly instead of being OOM-killed.

Measured (Flash-Next GSQ-IQ3_XXS, 1 GPU, -ncmoe 48, q8_0, the PLE flags, arms interleaved in one batch,
-n 512, MTP depth 4; t/s and tokens/step):

  depth (ctx)        dense    sparse    pool (204 MiB)   text (-n 64)
  5 298  (16 384)    72.09    71.58     71.27            pool == sparse: 332 chars dd1981213373
  102 899 (131 072)  59.10    54.70     54.65            (5.3k / 51 475 references; dense differs)
  180 035 (196 608)  48.94    43.16     43.41            pool == sparse: 861 chars 263c7f2be4d9

The pool lands ON the sparse ceiling at every depth (+0.5 / +0.6 / +0.58 %), so its placement is free and what
remains against a resident KV (-7 % / -11 %) is the sparse decode arm's own indexer cost (block 11), not the
KV's storage.  A 204 MiB pool replaces the resident attention KV's 1 632 MiB at 103k / 2 448 MiB at 180k, and
the freed VRAM goes to the expert arena: 103k plain decode, -n 2000, 23 579.8 -> 25 018.6 MiB (+1 438.8 MiB,
+18 slots/table), expert hit 96.26 -> 97.02 %, cold reads 42.0 -> 31.6 GiB.  The pool's own cost is 0.10-0.11
ms/token (mark/claim/map+fill) with fills 0.03-0.05 ms/step at 92-99 % hits, which is why a better page policy
(LRU / F-LRU / a heat prior) is measured dead at this shape.  Full numbers and the three-depth table:
../wip/kv-residency/BA-POOL-DESIGN.md §7-§8.

PURITY (the pool).  Greedy text pool == the resident sparse arm at 5.3k (332 dd1981213373), 51 475 depth
(854 43b30de151b9) and 180k (861 263c7f2be4d9), with the dense arm differing as documented (GREEDY-PURITY
§45/§63); LLAMA_KV_POOL_VERIFY=1 0 mismatches on every arm; gate-prefill-logits --ab "LLAMA_KV_REGION_POOL=..."
0.000000 / 100.000 % on the pool arm (base PPL 2.9225, bit-identical to the campaign's own base);
gate-park-idle-slots, gate-ckpt-reuse and gate-state-pipe PASS on a pooled cache (the mirror is authoritative,
which is what makes them trivial); test-backend-ops -o FLASH_ATTN_QSA pass; no GPU faults; and `--fit` accepts
the arms that only fit with the pool (-ncmoe 21/22 at -c 262144: refused without it, accepted with it; -fitp
moves 3 264 MiB from the device row to Host).

==========================================================================
PART 3 -- a STREAMED cache takes the sparse decode arm (B2)
==========================================================================

A cache that is streaming at the depth it reads -- a window the used extent has passed, or a pool -- takes
the sparse QSA decode arm; a resident cache keeps the dense one.  llama_kv_cache::is_streaming() plus the
qwen4exp arm policy, LLAMA_QSA_DENSE_DECODE_STREAM=0 to restore the old behaviour.  Measured on a spilled KV
(Flash-Next IQ3_XXS, -ncmoe 48, q8_0, 1 GPU, -n 400, 2 reps): +34.3 % at 102 899 depth (23.42 -> 31.43 t/s)
and +47.3 % at 180 035 (16.51 -> 24.31); a resident cache byte-identical (853 77a40bf40be1).
This is a decode-ARM choice, i.e. a different attention regime (like GREEDY-PURITY §45/§63), so a streamed
arm's text differs from the old dense-arm text by construction and a resident arm is the oracle.

==========================================================================
DEFAULT OFF, for both placements
==========================================================================

Neither knob is a speed feature on a config whose KV fits: the window costs the depth below W (11-26 %) and
the pool costs the sparse-vs-dense gap (-7 % at 103k, -11 % at 180k).  They are FITTABILITY features -- they
turn an OOM into a run and they hand the freed VRAM to the expert arena -- so both are opt-in and both stay
byte-identical, including the OOM, with their knobs unset.  Do not "fix" the defaults.

Goal C (the MTP drafter ring, TODO #121) joins the same block -- it is the same claim applied to the draft
context, and its default follows the delivery's own rule because it measures positive.

`LLAMA_SPEC_DRAFT_WINDOW` gives the MTP draft context a sliding window instead of a `-c`-sized KV: unset or
`auto` is the adaptive policy (a quarter of the context, clamped to [32768, 65536] cells), `0` is off
(byte-identical to a build without it) and `N` is a cell count.  The drafter reads its last few thousand
tokens, so a window at or above that is free and one far below it costs 7-9 % of decode; the clamps keep it
above that band, and the target verifies every token it proposes, so a truncated draft can only be slower.
The ring is the shipped reclaim in `llama_kv_cache::find_slot` (`n_swa`), not a new allocator, and the derived
kq mask stays valid -- its predicate compares each cell's POSITION, so a recycled cell reports its new one.

The VRAM win needed one more piece: an embedded MTP head has no GGUF of its own, so the slab's draft reserve
was the flat 3584 MiB and the freed VRAM never reached the expert arena.  For an embedded MTP head with an
active window the reserve is now derived from the model's own metadata with the weights zeroed (they are the
target's) and the KV sized by the window: aux 3.50 -> 0.76 GiB, i.e. the arena gains ~2.8 GiB.  A separate
MTP draft GGUF (qwen4exp/Flash-Next) and an un-windowed run keep the flat constant exactly as before, and a
declined arena preflight now re-evaluates when the smaller real value arrives instead of latching on the
load-time estimate.

