r194 corner fixes (found by a configuration battery against Flash-Next after r193):
* LLAMA_KV_REGION_POOL is capped at the cache's own cell count.  =1000000 on a 262144-cell context used to
  take a 12451 MiB device pool of 250000 pages where only 65536 blocks exist (never claimed, arena -4 GiB);
  it is now 3264 MiB / 65536 pages, and a small context clamps the same way.
* the draft KV window sizes only the reserve of the cache it actually windows.  A separate -md MTP head
  (Flash-Next) keeps its full -c cache, but the reserve was derived with the window: 921 MiB where the
  honest number is 1566 (kv 217 vs 862) at -c 262144, the under-charge direction.  Embedded heads
  (qwen3.5/3.6/3.8) are unchanged (776 MiB on the 35B).
The VRAM cliff at -c 262144 is documented (not code): the 768 MiB default headroom is one hipBLASLt
workspace short for the first prefill, GGML_CUDA_SLAB_HEADROOM_MIB=1536 is 3/3 requests clean.

Amendment (TODO #129's sibling): `-nkvo` declines the window, and the streamed arm keys on the placement.

The window is a DEVICE region plus a pinned host tail, but its guard did not test `offload`: with
`-nkvo` the per-layer buffer is chosen inside `if (offload)` and stays plain pageable host memory, while
the plan block still logged "WINDOWED KV ENABLED", reserved pinned-RAM spill budget and left
`kv_resident_cells` set.  `is_streaming()` then keyed the streamed (sparse) decode arm on that plan --
qwen4exp consumes it -- for a KV with no region at all.  Measured: `-nkvo LLAMA_KV_RESIDENT_CELLS=64`
built a plain `CPU KV buffer` of the whole context while announcing a 64-cell VRAM window.  The window
now declines once, with the reason (the pool already declines on `!offload`), so `-nkvo` means "no
streamed placement"; `is_streaming()` tests `kv_region`, the placement actually taken, which also covers
a device cache whose region buft the backend refused.  Text is unchanged in every arm (the decline is a
no-op: `-nkvo` + window already read as `-nkvo` alone).

Amendment (issue #264): the draft window is PER SEQUENCE, and under `--kv-unified` its cell count is
`(win + one batch) x n_seq_max`.

A unified cache holds every sequence in ONE stream, so the r193 sizing (`win + n_ubatch` cells) was shared
by all of them: once the first sequence had filled its window the tail was all the room left, the tail is
where that sequence's own ring writes, and a second sequence's first batch had no contiguous run to be
placed in -- `find_slot` refused, and every request on that slot answered `decode() failed: failed to
process speculative batch` (HTTP 500) while the drafter ran.  Reported on `--kv-unified -np 2` with a
65 536-cell window at `-c 262144` after slot 0 held ~100k tokens; reproduced here with a forced 8192-cell
window.  A per-stream cache (no `--kv-unified`) already sized each stream separately and is unchanged.

`llama_spec_draft_kv_cells()` is now the one geometry function -- the cache (`create_memory`), the slab's
draft reserve estimate and the "is a window active" test in `common` all call it, so the reserve can never
under-charge an allocation.  It grants `win` per sequence plus one placeable batch (`(win + n_ubatch) *
n_seq_max`, capped at the context), and DECLINES the window when that does not fit the context per sequence
rather than lowering it to `per_seq - n_ubatch`: a window that small saves one batch of cells per sequence
and masks the drafter's newest cells to do it.  `llama_spec_draft_window_cells()` stays the policy (it is
the per-sequence window).  n_seq_max == 1 -- every `llama-cli`/`llama-bench` run -- measures exactly what it
did (cells 1536 for a 1024-cell window at `-c 8192`), so no single-sequence arm changes at all.

Measured (dense 27B, 1 GPU, `-np 2 --kv-unified`): the issue's scenario now answers both slots, and
`scripts/gate-spec-draft-window.sh` (new) drives 6 requests over 2 slots with a 1024-cell window -- both
rings wrapping, prompts full re-prefilled by swapping them between slots -- with output byte-identical to
`LLAMA_SPEC_DRAFT_WINDOW=0` on every step and draft acceptance identical (17/36, 23/23).  Greedy text
`plain == draft-mtp == draft-mtp(W=1024) == draft-mtp(W=0)` (876 chars, sha 8c8fd395402f) at `-c 16384`.

And the reserve now charges a per-STREAM (no `--kv-unified`) draft cache for every stream it allocates:
`n_seq_max x` the per-stream cell count.  Before, a multi-slot windowed MTP server without `--kv-unified`
(35B-A3B `-ncmoe 40 -np 2 -c 8192`, window 1024: 2 x 1536 = 3072 cells allocated, 1536 charged) under-charged
the slab's aux reserve by one stream's window -- the direction the reserve header calls dangerous.  Measured:
3072 cells reported on both the unified and the per-stream arm now.

==========================================================================
PART 4 -- `-sm tensor`: the window across a split (r204, `TODO` #124)
==========================================================================

A Meta (tensor-split) device carries no backend reg, so the r181 answer was "not provided" and the window was
declined on every split.  `TODO` #124 designed the fix on a one-GPU box (`archive/work/kv-residency/
A6-TENSOR-SPLIT-WINDOW.md`, changes C1-C5); it is implemented and measured here, on a 3x R9700 host, and the
decline is gone.

* `ggml_backend_meta_kv_region_buft / _is_kv_buft / _grow` (new, `ggml-backend.h`): the Meta buffer type over
  the per-child region buffer types (ALL children or nothing), the is-a-region predicate `--fit` needs (the CUDA
  predicate is an identity on the child's `alloc_buffer` and cannot answer for a Meta buffer type), and a grow
  that fans out to every child.  A child that cannot be reached is a REFUSAL (-1), never the 0 the CUDA hook
  returns for "not a region buffer": for a Meta buffer that 0 was a silent success on unmapped VA, i.e. a batch
  that page-faults the GPU.
* `llama_kv_cache` resolves capability, the buffer type, the spill ceiling and the link through the Meta side; a
  MIRRORED cache (dsv4 / HRM, `llama_model::kv_cache_split_mirrored()`) declines the window with its own
  message, because every device holds the whole cache there (`n_devices x W x cell_bytes`) and the pinned-spill
  plan is per logical cache.
* `common/fit.cpp` decides the window inside the tensor-split branch: charged at its PHYSICAL `W x cell_bytes`
  (what all devices commit together) against `min_i(target_i / ratio_i)`, which makes the aggregate check imply
  every device's (the loader shards the KV along the KV-head axis by the same ratio it shards the weights), and
  the Meta entry's margin is now the SUM of the children's `--fit-target`s (it was child 0's, so
  `(n_devices - 1) x target` silently vanished from the budget).  The corrected KV term applies ONLY where a
  window is asked for: a run without `LLAMA_KV_RESIDENT_CELLS` keeps this branch's old arithmetic exactly, so
  the split's pre-existing `n_devices x` KV over-count is untouched outside the window arm.

Four defects the arms found, fixed in the same block:

1. the init-time window commit was rounded up to a whole map unit past the tensor's own extent, and on a split
   each child is its own VA reservation -- the last tensor's inflated window was refused (`the KV window
   (4.00 MiB) could not be committed in VRAM on device 1`).  Clamped to the region's END, in whole units
   (clamping to the tensor's own extent was tried first and is wrong: it leaves a short mapping off the unit
   grid and the next slide refuses to flip).
2. `ensure_span` refused a request that covered several WHOLE mappings; one batch can advance the boundary by
   more than one unit, and a 1 MiB unit is floored to the driver's 2 MiB granularity, so the request covered two
   mappings and aborted the batch.  Whole mappings now flip one at a time; a request that covers a mapping only
   in PART is still refused (that loses bytes on this driver).
3. the batched state save issued a 2-D D2H per device and `hipMemcpy2DAsync` fails (`invalid argument`) when
   the range begins in a spilled region's pinned host mapping (the tensor-split checkpoint save aborted:
   `cache_k_l64 (view)`, 1024 B x 4782 rows).  The region-backed directions of `get/set_tensor_2d_async` now go
   through the region buffer's own copy, ordered behind the compute stream like the pool mirror (a blocking
   default-stream copy does not wait for it, and the state saved mid-batch is torn).
4. that fallback's "contiguous rows = one copy" shortcut ignored the caller's destination pitch, which for a
   split splice is the FULL row, wider than one device's slice: the state-pipe gate failed on every cycle of
   all four async arms until a tight block required BOTH pitches to equal the width.

Verified (gfx1201, `ROCBLAS_USE_HIPBLASLT=0` for every fresh-process pair): text byte-identical on
`win3 == ref3` (3 devices, 4B, `057ea27b1009`) and `win2 == ref2 == win2mtp == ref2mtp` (2 devices, 27B, a
5246-token prompt, `7a7430617465`; draft acceptance 0.46154 in both MTP arms), also at a 1 MiB map unit; the
spill is real on EVERY device (32 mapping flips each at 4 MiB, 128 at 1 MiB); a `-c 1048576` 2-device split
(64 GiB of f16 KV) runs only because of the window; the single-device and `-nkvo` arms are unchanged; `--fit`
on the split reports a WINDOWED cache and keeps the pinned `-c 524288` (plus the pinned-RAM ceiling's widen and
drop arms, and `LLAMA_KV_REGION=0` silent); coherence identical to the r203 build on four no-window arms
(3-GPU 4B, 2-GPU and 1-GPU 27B, 2-GPU host-expert 35B-A3B); prefill-logit 0.000000 / 100.000 %; Protocol A
four-axis R/P/C/K 1.91x / 2.43x / 2.58x / 2.74x (acceptance 0.5869 / 0.8391 / 0.9112 / 0.9920, the r90 shape);
`gate-spec-draft-window.sh` 6/6; `gate-state-pipe.sh` 2-GPU `-sm tensor` PASS with and without the window (the
windowed arm failed before (3) and (4)); `GGML_CUDA_VMM_REGION_SELFTEST=1` PASS with a new multi-mapping arm;
per-block build 1/1; `validate-set.sh` strict 16/16.  Gate: `scripts/gate-kv-window-split.sh`.  Record:
`benchmarks/2026-11-01-kv-window-tensor-split-124.md`.

TODO #269 (r221): a host pool whose capacity COVERS the tensor must never retire a slot for an expert it
just copied into the arena -- it is a MIRROR, and retiring there un-loads an expert that is still
resident (the comment above already says so for the surplus case).  One condition now covers both, and
it is what makes the decode remap's pool-slot cold map write-once after sizing.
